                <pb facs="0850" n="842" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg842"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.102 Tientsin/550: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Counselor of Embassy in
                        China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Peck</hi>), <hi rendition="#italic">at
                        Chungking</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-01-13T17:00:00-05:00">January 13, 1940—5
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5. Your 26, January 13, noon, in regard to silver at Tientsin. Department
                    approves your attitude as reported in the telegram under reference. Department’s
                    attitude is summarized in last two sentences of paragraph 1 of its telegram No.
                    300, October 5, 7 p.m., to Tokyo.<anchor n="96" xml:id="d910-f1"/> Department
                    desires to avoid participation in official discussion relating to the
                    disposition of the silver in question.</p>
                <p>Sent to Chungking. Repeated to Peiping. Peiping please mail to Tokyo.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
